 @SerBallister  Far Cry 5 is a seriously CPU heavy game. My laptop with an i5 Skylake and GTX 960M and my desktop Ivy Bridge Xeon (essentially a 3770) and GTX 970 SLI get similar performance. I can run just about the same settings on both systems @ 1080P, 640 shaders vs over 3000 on the desktop.

Crytek tends to use a lot of software effects instead of GPU accelerated hardware effects and I have to wonder if being a software solution if it isn't sending a ton of data back and forth between the CPU and GPU.

Similar to openCL, I wonder if it sometimes it boosts performance and in other situations the latency between the CPU and GPU actually slows performance vs a CPU or GPU only job.

 @christophervanzetta  sure that's great and all, but that's not the same as running the old games, which is what we're talking about. One of the cool things about PC is accumulating a large library over the years which still works and you don't need to pay again every 5 years to play it on your latest rig, like in consoles.

Hoever, sometimes a remaster is required, but hopefully it's an exception and not a rule.
There's nGlide for the old Glide API, there's DOSBOX for old DOS APIs and speed thottling, even DirectX drivers replicate old versions on top of the new ones

