 @Keivz  I have a 55" screen, a workaround is to play in borderless window mode, reduce the screen size a bit and reduce the resolution.

The image quality is roughly the same on a shrunken display going 1:1 on screen density for giving up a couple of inches and you can still enjoy decent frames.

It's not ideal, but it's a small compromise if you've got enough screen real estate to spare in the first place.  It worked a treat for RE3 Remake at full settings, went from dropping to the 50's to holding a solid 60 and with everything turned up to max.  Couldn't even do that at all at 4K, wasn't an option under any circumstances, so the image quality is arguably better.

 @JadigertheReal  This still doesn't disprove what im saying, Multi-threaded designs need to become a standard. More devs need easier access to it so even indies could make multi-threaded games easily. Even outside of games this needs to happen. My original point isn't whether consoles better than pc I couldn't give a shit, its atm 8 core or more isn't the majority there for developers aren't going to risk or even do multi-threading for the next gen games.

My point is consoles even on that 2013 jaguer cpu which is horrendous and outdated when it launched, is still playing AAA games in 2020 that aren't much behind the average PC's expected performance/graphics.

You would see a generation jump in game quality if game engines could get over the insane task of multi-threading and making it easier for devs to handle. Look at doom 2020 that game is a monster on the 3080 and is reflective of what the GPU is capable of. The 3080 on other games isn't as good not because the card is shit, but because the engines the games are built on can't keep up with this rapidly evolving tech.

 @cud0s  It is looking like RDNA 2 will have a performance per watt advantage over these Nvidia cards, the real question is RDNA 2 offers the same level of performance of the 3080 and feature set like it's own DLSS.

As for leaks, they seem to be all over the place on RDNA 2, one moment they are saying they are around 2080ti level, the next they are saying it's 50% faster than the 2080ti, I wouldn't take much stock in them just yet and we don't have long to wait to see the real results.

Also for me, energy use is a big factor in what I buy, especially if it ends up heating up my room lol.

 @paul1979uk2000  Don't equate power consumption to heat output, this is a videocard not an incandescent light bulb.
If a videocard pulls 100W, it will not output the same heat as a 100W incandescent bulb.
More like 10-30W of heat which shouldn't heat up your room a whole lot if you have decent ventilation.

 @alexmehler6765  I leave my PC on 24/7 and we pay over here in taco land around the equivalent to $15-20 US dollars every 2 months for the entire house.
I don't game all the time though, most of the day it will probably use 10-30 watts.
Electricity is generally cheap in most places, look out how much you pay per KW, if you pay 1 euro per KW that's way too much lol.

 @paul1979uk2000  Are you going to have the card at 100% load all the time? If not, then heat output shouldn't be a problem.
If you want something that uses less energy, then you could get a low/mid card, or just play with integrated graphics.
Until we manage to build everything out of superconductors, we're going to have to deal with heat dissipation.

 @GoldSrc_  Unlikely but as I observed on my brothers 3080, games that use around 70% or 80% was pushing the temps into the mid 70c and this is in winter, he ended up putting on a manual fan curve at 70% so the clock rates stop clocking down so much which they tend to do when in the 70c and above, now he's getting around 50c to 65c but it sounds a lot louder, another solution I told him that he could undervolt it.

Besides, the energy usage isn't the issue, the lack of progress is, we are talking about 30% more performance for 30% more energy on a smaller node shrink and new architecture, that is really poor when you think about it, it's a lot easier to get more performance if they just blast more juice at it which it feels like what Nvidia has done here for the most part.

Basically, considering the node shrink and new architecture, it should be getting this performance without drawing more power than the 20 series, that it's not suggested to me that this isn't that much of a new architecture over the 20 series but just a tweak over that with the power limits unlocked to blast it with more juice, that's how it looks overall, hence people calling it Fermi 2.0.

 @GoldSrc_  To me it just feels like the 30 series is basically the 20 series of cards but with a few changes and improvement and the power limits unlocked so they can blast it with more juice, the performance bump over the 20 series matches up quite well with the extra energy usage.

My brothers been messing about with undervolting and been getting some good results, it's reduced the tdp by around 80w and temps by 15c, performance is a little lower but only by a few frames, the trade off is well worth it.

He's still messing with hit to see how stable it is so might have to bump it up a little in time but so far things are doing quite well.

